1:# Compile-only build drivers: protocol research and `go-v1` recommendation
9:## Key takeaways
14:4. **Build before any installation mutation.** Resolve, validate, gate, and inspect caches first; build all misses in an operation-private staging area; then commit cache entries and all manager-owned project state as one rollback-capable transaction. Record the consumer only after the transaction commits; run garbage collection last.
19:## Research basis
31:## 1. Current protocol and manager behavior
33:### 1.1 Normative baseline
41:- The profile requires all non-mutating checks before materialization, a side-effect-free dry-run, rollback on materialization failure, and consumer/GC work last ([manager profile, lines 50–80](https://github.com/relux-works/curator-spec/blob/57c1f56846d221ecc55786bd3c2467ec32f11730/profiles/manager.md#L50-L80)).
48:### 1.2 Go manager (`curator`) at `origin/main`
56:- Context replacement is rollback-capable per directory, but the enclosing install is not one transaction across runtime, every context, shims, environment files, adapters, and consumer state ([installer, lines 353–455](https://github.com/relux-works/curator/blob/17804ceae42d3bb62abf6e8a9d9cbef6dd5370b8/internal/install/install.go#L353-L455); [marker replacement](https://github.com/relux-works/curator/blob/17804ceae42d3bb62abf6e8a9d9cbef6dd5370b8/internal/marker/marker.go#L299-L379)). A later failure can therefore leave earlier nodes changed.
60:### 1.3 Python manager (`cocoaskills`) at `origin/main`
68:- Materialization is per node and per target, not a project-wide rollback transaction ([installer, lines 193–280](https://github.com/ivanopcode/cocoaskills/blob/6fc2fd97dbdf40f5b0e46f846eaa0d78a1b33d12/src/csk/installer.py#L193-L280)).
71:### 1.4 Consequence for implementation planning
75:## 2. No-hooks threat model
77:### 2.1 Assets and adversary control
91:- manager-owned policy constants, staging directories, cache locks, and transaction journal;
96:### 2.2 Security invariant
109:### 2.3 Explicitly forbidden operations
122:## 3. Manifest contract
124:### 3.1 Recommended schema shape
182:### 3.2 Valid manifest example
215:### 3.3 Invalid package-controlled arguments
228:### 3.4 Semantic validation rules
243:12. A manager that cannot enforce the fixed environment, offline module mode, native target, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, and transaction rules must reject the build command rather than approximate it.
250:## 4. Fixed `go-v1` driver
252:### 4.1 Supported shape
266:### 4.2 Toolchain selection and identity
268:The manager resolves the Go executable before entering any package-controlled directory. The candidate must be bundled with the manager or selected by trusted operator configuration/environment; it must not come from the repository, `runtime_roots`, project `.agents/bin`, the user `PATH`, or the package manifest. Resolve an outside launcher symlink and require the result to be the regular executable `<resolved-goroot>/bin/go` (`go.exe` on Windows). The candidate cannot be a wrapper or an executable outside the tree being fingerprinted. With `GOROOT` initially absent from the clean environment, the later `go env GOROOT` probe must canonicalize to the same derived root.
300:### 4.3 Environment construction
342:### 4.4 Exact process construction
382:### 4.5 Module and network behavior
397:### 4.6 cgo
403:### 4.7 Determinism claim
407:## 5. Cache identity and receipts
409:### 5.1 Why `skill + commit` is insufficient
422:### 5.2 Build input identity and cache key
559:### 5.3 Immutable cache layout
571:### 5.4 Receipt example
642:### 5.5 Install marker v2
685:### 5.6 Garbage collection
687:GC marks build cache keys referenced by all valid marker v2 files and in-flight transaction journals. It then sweeps unreferenced entries older than the normal grace period. If a marker cannot be parsed safely, GC retains potentially related entries and reports the uncertainty. It never uses receipt content alone as proof of a live consumer.
689:## 6. Lifecycle, ordering, dry-run, and rollback
691:### 6.1 Normative ordering
701:7. For a real operation, retain the private root, add one staging tree and transaction journal, and for every cache miss in provider-first/node-command order run the fixed full-graph `go list` preflight followed by fixed `go build` against that same validated raw snapshot. Do not publish anything yet; any snapshot change aborts the operation.
705:11. Commit those targets using an explicit rollback journal and deterministic order. Existing targets are backed up until every swap and the consumer-ledger update succeeds.
706:12. On success, remove backups and transaction state. Run stale-cache/runtime GC last; GC failure is a reported maintenance warning and does not corrupt the committed installation.
710:### 6.2 Dry-run
746:- retain the private telemetry/config probe root after return, or create `GOCACHE`, `GOMODCACHE`, temp build directories, fingerprint memos, audit records, registry response caches, runtime/build cache entries, transaction journals, shims, markers, or consumer records;
752:### 6.3 Rollback rules
756:- Newly published cache entries are removed on rollback if still unreferenced and owned by this transaction. If a concurrent successful transaction has begun referencing an identical valid entry, it is retained and will be governed by GC.
757:- Existing valid immutable cache entries are never modified during rollback.
758:- Backups are retained until rollback succeeds. An interrupted transaction is recovered under the same project lock on the next manager start before any new plan runs.
759:- The built artifact is never used as a rollback program or verifier.
761:This is a project-wide transaction requirement, not merely per-skill directory replacement.
